.SH "BUGS"
The startup validation block runs unconditionally when the file is sourced\. This means \fBRECORD_START_PROGRAM\fR and \fBRECORD_DBTYPE\fR must be exported before sourcing even in contexts where the PID\-file logic is not needed\.
.P
\fBexit \-1\fR is used throughout for error exits\. POSIX does not guarantee the behaviour of negative exit codes; most shells interpret \fB\-1\fR as 255, but this is not portable\.
.P
\fBcheck_for_errors\fR takes a single unquoted filename argument\. Filenames containing spaces or shell metacharacters must be quoted by the caller, and passing multiple files is not supported (unlike \fBrecord\-look\-for\-errors\.sh\fR which accepts \fB$@\fR)\.
.P
\fBget_stderr\fR relies on \fB/proc/<pid>/fd/2\fR, which is Linux\-specific and will not work on macOS or other non\-Linux systems\.
.P
\fBpidtree\fR uses a \fBdeclare \-A\fR associative array, which requires bash 4\.0 or later (or zsh)\. It will fail silently on bash 3\.x (the default on macOS)\.
.P
The PID file validation reads the file but takes no action if the PID is valid \(em it neither checks whether a process with that PID is actually running, nor prevents a second instance of the workflow from starting\.
